    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "03de6879_6f3f29b0",
        "filename": "drivers/scsi/ufs/ufshci.h",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 332,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137063
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-03T22:45:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m pretty sure this is broken on big endian CPUs, because the words are being being read and written from the memory-mapped registers using readl() and writel() which convert to/from native endianness.  Thus the value stored in .reg_val will actually be u32, not __le32.\n\nSo either we need something like this:\n\n union ufs_crypto_capabilities {\n        u32 reg_val;\n        struct {\n #ifdef __LITTLE_ENDIAN\n                u8 num_crypto_cap;\n                u8 config_count;\n                u8 reserved;\n                u8 config_array_ptr;\n #else\n                u8 config_array_ptr;\n                u8 reserved;\n                u8 config_count;\n                u8 num_crypto_cap;\n #endif\n        };\n};\n\n\n... or we don\u0027t declare the byte fields and instead read them from reg_val using shifts and masking, or we actually make it always be __le32 in memory.\n\nSame for ufs_crypto_cap_entry and ufs_crypto_cfg_entry.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 324,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 332,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "b1742a54728c92581868f40279419d308d5b88e9",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
